{"source":"github","project_id":"bindrcpp","target":"krlmlr/bindrcpp","id":"pr/89","ref":89,"title":"ci: Document with a patched roxygen2 that keeps the sentence gap","description":"Split out of #87 so it can be decided on its own.\n\n## The problem\n\ncommonmark discards the whitespace a line break stands for. roxygen2 turns every softbreak into a bare newline, so the `.Rd` carries a line break with nothing after it and `Rd2txt()` renders it as a single space. Prose written one sentence per line therefore comes out single-spaced, while the same words wrapped to a fixed width with two spaces after the period keep the gap.\n\nThat makes the two styles mutually exclusive: splitting a sentence pair separated by two spaces silently drops one of them. Across the fleet that forced single roxygen lines of 300, 460 and 600 columns, because joining was the only way to keep the gap.\n\nOnly the text renderer is affected. `Rd2HTML()` and `Rd2latex()` differ by whitespace that HTML and TeX collapse — and LaTeX applies its own sentence spacing regardless. But text is what `?topic` shows, so it is the version most people read.\n\n## The change\n\nA new composite action, `.github/workflows/roxygen2-fork`, installs roxygen2 from upstream with the `R/` part of `krlmlr/roxygen2@f-sentence-spacing` applied on top, and `R-CMD-check.yaml` runs it immediately before the Roxygenize step.\n\nIt shallow-clones upstream, fetches the branch, computes the diff against their merge base restricted to `R/`, and applies it with `git apply --3way`. Restricting to `R/` means an unrelated upstream change to a test fixture cannot fail the documentation step. It aborts if the patch is empty or does not apply, rather than silently installing an unpatched build, and afterwards asserts that what it installed really carries the patch.\n\n`Config/roxygen2/version` becomes `8.1.0.9100`. The `.9100` suffix distinguishes a patched build from upstream's own `.9000` development builds; if upstream moves, the `x.y.z` part follows it and the suffix stays. `DESCRIPTION` is DCF and cannot carry a comment line — `read.dcf()` rejects one as malformed — so the explanation lives in a `Config/cynkra/roxygen2` field.\n\n## Why it is a separate pull request\n\nTaking it changes rendered help across the package; leaving it means the reformatting below it renders exactly as the package renders today, with one space between sentences. Neither half needs the other to be correct, so they should be able to be decided separately.\n\n`man/` is not edited by hand here. CI roxygenises and commits the regenerated `.Rd` files itself, so they arrive on this branch as an automated commit rather than in the hand-written diff, which stays reviewable.\n\n## Upstream\n\nThe roxygen2 side is `krlmlr/roxygen2#8`, on the fork, not yet filed with r-lib. It is deliberately narrow: mid-sentence breaks are untouched, links are unaffected, and two guards keep it from firing wrongly — an abbreviation list so a line ending in `e.g.` or `et al.` does not gain a mid-sentence gap, and a fix for a determinism bug where a line starting with `\\doi{}` or `\\code{}` gained or lost its gap at random, because the test was reading roxygen2's own random placeholder for protected Rd tags.\n\nPart of a fleet-wide pass; the prototype is cynkra/cynkratemplate#131.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01WWhverMTZZKgEpUuTK117m\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_01WWhverMTZZKgEpUuTK117m)_","published_at":"2026-09-13T18:27:23Z","url":"https://github.com/krlmlr/bindrcpp/pull/89","type":"pr","extra":{"author":"krlmlr","state":"OPEN"}}
{"source":"github","project_id":"bindrcpp","target":"krlmlr/bindrcpp","id":"pr/88","ref":88,"title":"docs: State the goals and non-goals","description":"Evidence:\n\n- **\"no separate R interface\"** — `create_env()` and `populate_env()` are re-exported from bindr verbatim in `R/bindr.R`. That is the boundary against bindr: this package is the C++ half, nothing more.\n- **\"read-only bindings\"** — inherited from bindr's contract.\n- **\"no non-Rcpp interface\"** — `Imports` and `LinkingTo` are Rcpp, and every typedef in `bindrcpp_types.h` is an Rcpp type.\n- **\"no movement beyond the current interface\"** — `NEWS.md` after 0.2.2 (2018) records compatibility and build fixes only.\n\n**One thing I deliberately did not write:** anything calling this package superseded. It carries no lifecycle badge at all and no statement anywhere says it has been replaced, so the maintenance state is expressed only as what the record shows — a fixed interface and NEWS entries that are fixes rather than features — rather than as a judgement I am not entitled to make. If you *do* consider it superseded, that belongs in a lifecycle badge and a sentence you write, not inferred by me.\n\nOnly `README.Rmd` and `README.md` change — no `index.md`, which is correct here, since the README and front page would be identical. Rendered twice, byte-identical.\n\nStacked on #87, which this PR targets.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01WWhverMTZZKgEpUuTK117m\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_01WWhverMTZZKgEpUuTK117m)_","published_at":"2026-09-13T11:19:04Z","url":"https://github.com/krlmlr/bindrcpp/pull/88","type":"pr","extra":{"author":"krlmlr","state":"OPEN"}}
{"source":"github","project_id":"bindrcpp","target":"krlmlr/bindrcpp","id":"pr/87","ref":87,"title":"docs: Break lines at meaning boundaries","description":"Reformatting only, no wording changes. Prose in `README.Rmd` and the roxygen under `R/` now breaks at sentence and clause boundaries rather than wrapping to a fixed width.\n\nThe payoff is sentence-level diffs. A reworded sentence touches one line instead of reflowing the paragraph around it, so review sees the change and not the rewrap.\n\n`man/` is not edited by hand here. roxygen2 passes source line breaks straight through to the `.Rd`, and the regenerated files are generated output: CI roxygenises and commits them itself, so they arrive on this branch as an automated commit rather than in the hand-written diff. The rendered help does move, in exactly one way: where a two-space sentence gap was split across lines it narrows to one space. `tools::Rd2txt()` over the topics whose `.Rd` changed puts 1 of 1 in that position — in `bindrcpp-package.Rd`, `character vector.  Access` becomes `character vector. Access`. Restoring those gaps is what the patched roxygen2 further up the stack does. `render_readme()` run twice produced byte-identical output. No `index.md` is created — this package's front page and README would be identical, which is the existing arrangement.\n\nThe `create_env_string()` bullet in `R/bindrcpp-package.R` keeps its original wrapping. Its two sentences are separated by a double space, which `Rd2txt()` renders literally, so breaking there would change the rendered help. The other bullets and the `@details` lead-in were reflowed normally.\n\nThe generation notice at the top of `README.Rmd` was itself wrapped to a fixed width, so two of its sentences started mid-line. It now carries one sentence per line, keeping the five-space continuation indent, matching the form DBI, dm, igraphdata and wrswoR already have. The notice is copied into the rendered file, so `README.md` is re-rendered along with it; the wording is word-for-word unchanged.\n\n`R/RcppExports.R` is left alone — generated, and its header says so.\n\nOne consequence is worth stating plainly. A line break which follows the end of a sentence currently renders as one space, not two, so writing one sentence per line drops the sentence gap from the rendered help. Restoring it needs a patched roxygen2, which is a separate decision: it is #89, stacked on top of this chain, and can be taken or left on its own. Without it this change simply renders as the package renders today.\n\nPart of a fleet-wide pass. r-lib/here#195 is the reference PR, reviewed by hand before the rest were run.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01WWhverMTZZKgEpUuTK117m","published_at":"2026-09-13T10:25:01Z","updated_at":"2026-09-26T14:44:33Z","url":"https://github.com/krlmlr/bindrcpp/pull/87","type":"pr","extra":{"author":"krlmlr","merged_at":"2026-09-26T14:44:33Z","state":"MERGED"}}
{"source":"github","project_id":"bindrcpp","target":"krlmlr/bindrcpp","id":"pr/86","ref":86,"title":"docs: Harmonize README and pkgdown front page rendering","description":"Part of a fleet-wide pkgdown harmonization. Stacked on #84 (the `pak::pak()` README change), which this PR targets, so any conflict surfaces here rather than at merge time. The output format lands in cynkra/cynkratemplate#116, and the CI step that will keep these files current in cynkra/cynkratemplate#118.\n\n## What changes\n\n`README.Rmd` declares `output: cynkratemplate::readme_document` — a `github_document` that also writes the pkgdown front page from the same render, with `-smart` and `--wrap=preserve` so diffs stay sentence-level. Stating the format by name rather than repeating its settings means the settings live in one place instead of thirty.\n\n**No `index.md` is written here, and that is the correct outcome.** This README has no horizontal rule and emits no ANSI colour, so the front page and the README would be identical; the format drops the file rather than commit a duplicate, and pkgdown falls through to `README.md` on its own lookup order. `.Rbuildignore` is kept in step either way, so nothing is left dangling.\n\nThe `README.md` diff is therefore the pandoc settings taking effect: `--wrap=preserve` stops lines being rebroken at 72 columns, `-smart` leaves quotes and dashes as written. No prose changes.\n\n## Reproducing\n\n```r\nrmarkdown::render(\"README.Rmd\")   # or devtools::build_readme(), or Knit\n```\n\nTwo consecutive renders are byte-identical — checked before committing, because cynkra/cynkratemplate#118 re-renders in CI and commits the result.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01WWhverMTZZKgEpUuTK117m\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_01WWhverMTZZKgEpUuTK117m)_","published_at":"2026-09-13T10:03:05Z","updated_at":"2026-09-26T14:44:32Z","url":"https://github.com/krlmlr/bindrcpp/pull/86","type":"pr","extra":{"author":"krlmlr","merged_at":"2026-09-26T14:44:32Z","state":"MERGED"}}
{"source":"github","project_id":"bindrcpp","target":"krlmlr/bindrcpp","id":"pr/84","ref":84,"title":"docs: Use `pak::pak()` for the development install","description":"One of the items `usethis::use_tidy_upkeep_issue()` prescribes for 2023, applied across the package fleet as a single-concern change per repository.\n\n`pak::pak()` resolves system requirements and installs binaries where they exist, so it does not need a compiler for pure-R dependencies the way `install_github()` does.\n\nThe `# install.packages(\"pak\")` comment is kept, matching the packages in this fleet that already use pak. pak is not a dependency of usethis, and only devtools imports it (`pak (\u003e= 0.9.5)`), so a reader with neither installed still needs that line.\n\nBoth `README.Rmd` and the generated `README.md` are updated in step. The snippet sits in a non-evaluated code block in both, so the edit is textually identical and no rebuild was needed.\n\nPart of a fleet-wide upkeep pass.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01WWhverMTZZKgEpUuTK117m\n","published_at":"2026-09-12T17:52:27Z","updated_at":"2026-09-26T14:44:31Z","url":"https://github.com/krlmlr/bindrcpp/pull/84","type":"pr","extra":{"author":"krlmlr","merged_at":"2026-09-26T14:44:31Z","state":"MERGED"}}
